1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,946
How do you raise almost two billion

2
00:00:02,946 --> 00:00:03,783
dollars,

3
00:00:03,783 --> 00:00:06,639
hire the biggest stars in Hollywood,

4
00:00:06,639 --> 00:00:08,946
and still shut down six months after

5
00:00:08,946 --> 00:00:09,835
launch?

6
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:12,343
The app was called Quibi.

7
00:00:12,413 --> 00:00:14,768
It raised one point seven five billion

8
00:00:14,768 --> 00:00:18,193
dollars before it had a single customer.

9
00:00:18,503 --> 00:00:20,650
It launched in April twenty twenty and

10
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:23,593
announced it was shutting down in October.

11
00:00:23,713 --> 00:00:25,685
And the strangest part is that the

12
00:00:25,685 --> 00:00:28,151
idea behind it was not crazy at

13
00:00:28,151 --> 00:00:28,997
all.

14
00:00:39,650 --> 00:00:42,842
What exactly were investors buying?

15
00:00:48,650 --> 00:00:51,430
The idea came from Jeffrey Katzenberg,

16
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:53,725
the former chairman of Disney's film

17
00:00:53,725 --> 00:00:54,465
studio,

18
00:00:54,465 --> 00:00:55,715
with Meg Whitman,

19
00:00:55,715 --> 00:00:58,240
the former head of eBay and HP,

20
00:00:58,240 --> 00:01:00,370
as chief executive.

21
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:01,530
The name meant quick bites.

22
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:03,478
Hollywood quality shows,

23
00:01:03,478 --> 00:01:06,682
cut into episodes of ten minutes or

24
00:01:06,682 --> 00:01:07,151
less,

25
00:01:07,151 --> 00:01:09,230
made only for your phone.

26
00:01:09,380 --> 00:01:11,401
Something to watch on the bus,

27
00:01:11,401 --> 00:01:12,287
in a queue,

28
00:01:12,287 --> 00:01:14,022
or between meetings.

29
00:01:14,283 --> 00:01:16,293
Hollywood loved it.

30
00:01:16,293 --> 00:01:17,850
In twenty eighteen,

31
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:20,488
the first funding round alone raised one

32
00:01:20,488 --> 00:01:22,143
billion dollars.

33
00:01:22,153 --> 00:01:24,518
The investors read like a guest list

34
00:01:24,518 --> 00:01:27,343
of the entertainment industry.

35
00:01:27,343 --> 00:01:28,135
Disney,

36
00:01:28,135 --> 00:01:29,474
NBCUniversal,

37
00:01:29,474 --> 00:01:30,266
Sony,

38
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:31,605
WarnerMedia,

39
00:01:31,605 --> 00:01:32,397
Viacom,

40
00:01:32,397 --> 00:01:33,189
Lionsgate,

41
00:01:33,189 --> 00:01:33,708
MGM,

42
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:36,713
plus Alibaba and the banks Goldman Sachs

43
00:01:36,713 --> 00:01:38,133
and JPMorgan.

44
00:01:38,263 --> 00:01:39,535
And before launch,

45
00:01:39,535 --> 00:01:42,131
advertisers bought its entire first year

46
00:01:42,131 --> 00:01:42,884
of ads,

47
00:01:42,884 --> 00:01:45,220
worth one hundred and fifty million

48
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:46,339
dollars.

49
00:01:46,583 --> 00:01:48,443
There was a warning sign.

50
00:01:48,443 --> 00:01:50,767
The same summer Quibi raised its first

51
00:01:50,767 --> 00:01:51,441
billion,

52
00:01:51,441 --> 00:01:53,998
the phone company Verizon shut down its

53
00:01:53,998 --> 00:01:56,299
own short video service for phones,

54
00:01:56,299 --> 00:01:58,293
called Go Ninety.

55
00:01:58,633 --> 00:02:00,556
It had never found an audience,

56
00:02:00,556 --> 00:02:02,716
and Verizon wrote off six hundred and

57
00:02:02,716 --> 00:02:04,207
fifty-eight million dollars,

58
00:02:04,207 --> 00:02:06,103
mostly because of it.

59
00:02:06,413 --> 00:02:08,807
Katzenberg insisted his service would be

60
00:02:08,807 --> 00:02:10,744
fundamentally different,

61
00:02:10,744 --> 00:02:12,485
built from the ground up for watching

62
00:02:12,485 --> 00:02:13,738
on a phone.

63
00:02:13,983 --> 00:02:16,103
Let me draw the bet.

64
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:17,204
On one side,

65
00:02:17,204 --> 00:02:18,405
a huge number:

66
00:02:18,405 --> 00:02:20,714
billions of people with phones and a

67
00:02:20,714 --> 00:02:23,393
few spare minutes every day.

68
00:02:23,663 --> 00:02:24,917
On the other side,

69
00:02:24,917 --> 00:02:25,958
a simple price,

70
00:02:25,958 --> 00:02:27,833
a few dollars a month.

71
00:02:28,353 --> 00:02:30,053
If even a small slice of those

72
00:02:30,053 --> 00:02:30,881
people paid,

73
00:02:30,881 --> 00:02:33,393
the business would be enormous.

74
00:02:33,413 --> 00:02:34,903
That was the math.

75
00:02:35,063 --> 00:02:36,766
The question was whether people would

76
00:02:36,766 --> 00:02:39,226
actually pay for minutes they were already

77
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:40,583
filling for free.

78
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:41,436
Then,

79
00:02:41,436 --> 00:02:42,947
just before launch,

80
00:02:42,947 --> 00:02:46,324
the whole world stopped commuting.

81
00:02:51,983 --> 00:02:54,260
What happens to an app for busy

82
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:57,138
people when nobody is busy?

83
00:03:00,983 --> 00:03:02,975
Quibi launched on April sixth,

84
00:03:02,975 --> 00:03:04,072
twenty twenty,

85
00:03:04,072 --> 00:03:06,534
just as the pandemic was locking people

86
00:03:06,534 --> 00:03:08,253
inside their homes.

87
00:03:08,383 --> 00:03:10,804
It offered a free trial of ninety

88
00:03:10,804 --> 00:03:11,222
days,

89
00:03:11,222 --> 00:03:13,422
then cost four ninety-nine a month with

90
00:03:13,422 --> 00:03:16,223
ads or seven ninety-nine without.

91
00:03:16,403 --> 00:03:17,224
On launch day,

92
00:03:17,224 --> 00:03:19,752
according to the analytics firm Sensor

93
00:03:19,752 --> 00:03:20,363
Tower,

94
00:03:20,363 --> 00:03:22,469
it was downloaded about about three

95
00:03:22,469 --> 00:03:24,333
hundred thousand times.

96
00:03:24,353 --> 00:03:26,113
That sounds like a lot.

97
00:03:26,223 --> 00:03:28,571
But Disney's new streaming service had

98
00:03:28,571 --> 00:03:31,179
seen about four million installs at its

99
00:03:31,179 --> 00:03:32,301
own launch.

100
00:03:32,550 --> 00:03:34,750
The launch line-up was full of famous

101
00:03:34,750 --> 00:03:35,459
names,

102
00:03:35,459 --> 00:03:38,123
from Jennifer Lopez to Liam Hemsworth,

103
00:03:38,123 --> 00:03:40,811
with projects promised from directors like

104
00:03:40,811 --> 00:03:44,100
Steven Spielberg and Guillermo del Toro.

105
00:03:44,550 --> 00:03:47,114
Apple promoted the app across its store,

106
00:03:47,114 --> 00:03:49,054
and it climbed to number three on

107
00:03:49,054 --> 00:03:50,240
the App Store.

108
00:03:50,240 --> 00:03:51,259
For a few days,

109
00:03:51,259 --> 00:03:53,548
Quibi looked like the hit Hollywood had

110
00:03:53,548 --> 00:03:54,564
paid for.

111
00:03:54,817 --> 00:03:57,527
And Quibi had a bigger problem.

112
00:03:57,617 --> 00:03:59,867
It only worked on phones.

113
00:04:00,057 --> 00:04:02,231
People stuck at home wanted to watch

114
00:04:02,231 --> 00:04:04,887
on the big television in the living

115
00:04:04,887 --> 00:04:05,346
room,

116
00:04:05,346 --> 00:04:07,278
and at first Quibi could not be

117
00:04:07,278 --> 00:04:09,327
sent to a TV at all.

118
00:04:09,817 --> 00:04:11,623
Its showpiece technology,

119
00:04:11,623 --> 00:04:12,972
called Turnstyle,

120
00:04:12,972 --> 00:04:14,801
let you rotate your phone and switch

121
00:04:14,801 --> 00:04:17,545
between a horizontal and a vertical

122
00:04:17,545 --> 00:04:19,457
version of the same scene.

123
00:04:19,457 --> 00:04:20,477
Clever.

124
00:04:20,807 --> 00:04:22,597
But it was built for a commuter

125
00:04:22,597 --> 00:04:24,589
who no longer existed.

126
00:04:24,850 --> 00:04:27,177
Even that clever feature came with a

127
00:04:27,177 --> 00:04:28,010
fight.

128
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:29,037
Before launch,

129
00:04:29,037 --> 00:04:32,185
an interactive video company called Eko

130
00:04:32,185 --> 00:04:33,062
sued Quibi,

131
00:04:33,062 --> 00:04:35,536
alleging that Turnstyle infringed its

132
00:04:35,536 --> 00:04:38,160
patent and used its trade secrets.

133
00:04:38,280 --> 00:04:40,754
Quibi said the claims had absolutely no

134
00:04:40,754 --> 00:04:41,352
merit,

135
00:04:41,352 --> 00:04:43,001
and pointed out that it held its

136
00:04:43,001 --> 00:04:44,220
own patent.

137
00:04:44,310 --> 00:04:46,316
A legal battle was the last thing

138
00:04:46,316 --> 00:04:48,031
a new app needed.

139
00:04:48,283 --> 00:04:50,313
The downloads came.

140
00:04:50,313 --> 00:04:53,090
The question was whether anyone would

141
00:04:53,090 --> 00:04:53,999
stay.

142
00:04:59,650 --> 00:05:02,475
How many people actually paid?

143
00:05:08,650 --> 00:05:09,363
By May,

144
00:05:09,363 --> 00:05:12,311
CNN reported that Quibi had about about

145
00:05:12,311 --> 00:05:14,744
one point three million active users,

146
00:05:14,744 --> 00:05:16,464
most of them still on the free

147
00:05:16,464 --> 00:05:17,210
trial.

148
00:05:17,210 --> 00:05:19,139
Disney's service had more than fifty

149
00:05:19,139 --> 00:05:20,810
million customers.

150
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:22,932
Netflix had a hundred and eighty-three

151
00:05:22,932 --> 00:05:23,950
million.

152
00:05:23,950 --> 00:05:24,858
Next to them,

153
00:05:24,858 --> 00:05:27,322
Quibi was a rounding error.

154
00:05:27,583 --> 00:05:29,673
Then the trials ended.

155
00:05:29,673 --> 00:05:32,574
Sensor Tower estimated that of about about

156
00:05:32,574 --> 00:05:34,645
nine hundred and ten thousand people who

157
00:05:34,645 --> 00:05:36,489
signed up in the first few days,

158
00:05:36,489 --> 00:05:39,183
only about about seventy-two thousand kept

159
00:05:39,183 --> 00:05:41,233
paying after the free three months.

160
00:05:41,443 --> 00:05:43,603
That is about eight percent.

161
00:05:43,733 --> 00:05:45,924
Quibi said the estimate was wrong by

162
00:05:45,924 --> 00:05:47,656
an order of magnitude,

163
00:05:47,656 --> 00:05:49,628
and that five point six million people

164
00:05:49,628 --> 00:05:51,543
had downloaded the app.

165
00:05:51,993 --> 00:05:54,235
But it never published its number of

166
00:05:54,235 --> 00:05:55,650
paying subscribers.

167
00:05:55,917 --> 00:05:58,877
The advertisers noticed too.

168
00:05:58,997 --> 00:06:00,960
They had paid for short ads of

169
00:06:00,960 --> 00:06:01,493
six,

170
00:06:01,493 --> 00:06:03,428
ten or fifteen seconds,

171
00:06:03,428 --> 00:06:05,082
with no skip button,

172
00:06:05,082 --> 00:06:07,886
expecting a young audience glued to their

173
00:06:07,886 --> 00:06:09,047
phones.

174
00:06:09,157 --> 00:06:09,825
Instead,

175
00:06:09,825 --> 00:06:12,336
the audience was smaller than promised,

176
00:06:12,336 --> 00:06:15,077
and according to Broadcasting and Cable,

177
00:06:15,077 --> 00:06:16,689
the big brands that had bought one

178
00:06:16,689 --> 00:06:18,993
hundred and fifty million dollars of

179
00:06:18,993 --> 00:06:21,666
advertising were disappointed.

180
00:06:21,917 --> 00:06:24,180
Let me draw why a free trial

181
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:26,397
can hide a problem.

182
00:06:26,537 --> 00:06:28,177
Imagine a funnel.

183
00:06:28,287 --> 00:06:29,221
At the top,

184
00:06:29,221 --> 00:06:31,157
everyone who downloads.

185
00:06:31,157 --> 00:06:32,272
In the middle,

186
00:06:32,272 --> 00:06:34,137
those who try a few shows.

187
00:06:34,137 --> 00:06:35,269
At the bottom,

188
00:06:35,269 --> 00:06:36,885
the few who pay when the free

189
00:06:36,885 --> 00:06:37,947
months end.

190
00:06:38,267 --> 00:06:40,445
A free trial makes the top look

191
00:06:40,445 --> 00:06:41,217
huge.

192
00:06:41,217 --> 00:06:43,477
But a business lives on the bottom.

193
00:06:43,527 --> 00:06:45,416
Quibi had a wide top and a

194
00:06:45,416 --> 00:06:47,432
very narrow bottom.

195
00:06:47,683 --> 00:06:49,220
And all this time,

196
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:51,702
the money kept going out.

197
00:06:57,350 --> 00:07:00,375
Where did the money go?

198
00:07:06,350 --> 00:07:08,500
Mostly into shows.

199
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:10,893
Quibi paid Hollywood prices for stars and

200
00:07:10,893 --> 00:07:11,710
directors,

201
00:07:11,710 --> 00:07:14,013
for episodes that were only a few

202
00:07:14,013 --> 00:07:15,450
minutes long.

203
00:07:15,840 --> 00:07:18,017
According to reports in June twenty

204
00:07:18,017 --> 00:07:18,649
twenty,

205
00:07:18,649 --> 00:07:21,044
the company expected to have spent one

206
00:07:21,044 --> 00:07:23,221
billion dollars of its money by the

207
00:07:23,221 --> 00:07:23,852
autumn,

208
00:07:23,852 --> 00:07:25,812
and that it would need another two

209
00:07:25,812 --> 00:07:27,989
hundred million dollars by mid twenty

210
00:07:27,989 --> 00:07:29,677
twenty-one to keep going.

211
00:07:29,917 --> 00:07:31,721
And there was a catch in the

212
00:07:31,721 --> 00:07:32,837
contracts.

213
00:07:32,997 --> 00:07:35,496
Quibi's deals with the creators gave it

214
00:07:35,496 --> 00:07:38,272
exclusive rights to each show for only

215
00:07:38,272 --> 00:07:39,427
two years.

216
00:07:39,427 --> 00:07:41,885
So it was spending fortunes on shows

217
00:07:41,885 --> 00:07:43,851
it would not keep to itself for

218
00:07:43,851 --> 00:07:44,697
long.

219
00:07:44,807 --> 00:07:46,507
When it came time to sell,

220
00:07:46,507 --> 00:07:48,701
a buyer would get a short window,

221
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:50,533
not a library.

222
00:07:50,783 --> 00:07:52,943
Quibi tried to adapt.

223
00:07:53,083 --> 00:07:54,821
It added ways to cast shows to

224
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:55,452
the TV,

225
00:07:55,452 --> 00:07:58,276
and in October it finally released apps

226
00:07:58,276 --> 00:07:59,963
for televisions.

227
00:08:00,133 --> 00:08:01,132
In September,

228
00:08:01,132 --> 00:08:03,172
with more than two hundred million dollars

229
00:08:03,172 --> 00:08:04,171
still in the bank,

230
00:08:04,171 --> 00:08:06,393
it started looking for a buyer.

231
00:08:06,393 --> 00:08:08,472
It did not find one.

232
00:08:08,717 --> 00:08:11,137
What would a buyer even get?

233
00:08:11,257 --> 00:08:12,881
According to Recode,

234
00:08:12,881 --> 00:08:14,269
the pitch was some cash,

235
00:08:14,269 --> 00:08:17,094
the Turnstyle technology for rotating

236
00:08:17,094 --> 00:08:17,776
video,

237
00:08:17,776 --> 00:08:19,659
and a slate of shows that Quibi

238
00:08:19,659 --> 00:08:22,377
would only control for a short time.

239
00:08:22,697 --> 00:08:24,531
For a big streaming company,

240
00:08:24,531 --> 00:08:26,180
it was cheaper to wait and see

241
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:28,035
what would be left after the shutdown

242
00:08:28,035 --> 00:08:30,489
than to buy the whole company.

243
00:08:30,750 --> 00:08:31,577
And so,

244
00:08:31,577 --> 00:08:34,115
barely six months after launch,

245
00:08:34,115 --> 00:08:36,997
the founders made a decision.

246
00:08:42,650 --> 00:08:45,053
When do you stop?

247
00:08:51,650 --> 00:08:53,615
On October twenty-first,

248
00:08:53,615 --> 00:08:54,833
twenty twenty,

249
00:08:54,833 --> 00:08:57,920
Quibi announced it was shutting down.

250
00:08:58,240 --> 00:09:00,550
Katzenberg said its standalone business

251
00:09:00,550 --> 00:09:02,830
model was no longer viable.

252
00:09:03,310 --> 00:09:05,711
The company still had around about three

253
00:09:05,711 --> 00:09:08,112
hundred and fifty million dollars in the

254
00:09:08,112 --> 00:09:08,527
bank,

255
00:09:08,527 --> 00:09:11,310
and about about two hundred employees.

256
00:09:11,650 --> 00:09:13,592
Instead of spending the rest,

257
00:09:13,592 --> 00:09:15,804
the founders chose to close and return

258
00:09:15,804 --> 00:09:17,771
as much money as they could to

259
00:09:17,771 --> 00:09:19,354
the investors.

260
00:09:19,617 --> 00:09:22,167
Katzenberg explained it simply.

261
00:09:22,217 --> 00:09:23,488
Keeping Quibi going,

262
00:09:23,488 --> 00:09:24,113
he said,

263
00:09:24,113 --> 00:09:26,437
would not have changed the outcome.

264
00:09:26,797 --> 00:09:28,699
It would only have spent a whole

265
00:09:28,699 --> 00:09:30,813
lot more money without any value to

266
00:09:30,813 --> 00:09:32,047
show for it.

267
00:09:32,337 --> 00:09:34,341
The founders said that in the weeks

268
00:09:34,341 --> 00:09:35,068
before,

269
00:09:35,068 --> 00:09:37,798
they had looked at every option,

270
00:09:37,798 --> 00:09:38,524
partners,

271
00:09:38,524 --> 00:09:40,377
buyers and new funding.

272
00:09:40,517 --> 00:09:42,570
Every one of those stones,

273
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:43,239
they said,

274
00:09:43,239 --> 00:09:44,531
was turned.

275
00:09:44,783 --> 00:09:45,446
At first,

276
00:09:45,446 --> 00:09:47,961
Katzenberg had blamed the pandemic for

277
00:09:47,961 --> 00:09:49,933
almost everything.

278
00:09:50,293 --> 00:09:51,968
In their farewell letter,

279
00:09:51,968 --> 00:09:54,053
the founders were more honest.

280
00:09:54,193 --> 00:09:55,746
Other businesses had faced the pandemic

281
00:09:55,746 --> 00:09:56,813
and found their way through it.

282
00:09:56,813 --> 00:09:58,856
They had not.

283
00:09:59,117 --> 00:10:01,263
In January twenty twenty-one,

284
00:10:01,263 --> 00:10:03,915
the streaming company Roku bought Quibi's

285
00:10:03,915 --> 00:10:07,167
library of more than seventy-five shows.

286
00:10:07,217 --> 00:10:09,583
The price was reportedly significantly

287
00:10:09,583 --> 00:10:11,957
less than one hundred million dollars.

288
00:10:12,127 --> 00:10:14,398
A company that had spent around a

289
00:10:14,398 --> 00:10:16,441
billion dollars sold its shows for a

290
00:10:16,441 --> 00:10:18,176
small fraction of that.

291
00:10:18,417 --> 00:10:20,917
So what killed Quibi?

292
00:10:20,917 --> 00:10:22,367
Not one thing.

293
00:10:22,367 --> 00:10:23,495
The pandemic,

294
00:10:23,495 --> 00:10:25,084
a phone-only design,

295
